==========================================
DAY 18 NOTES — SSH & Remote Access
Date: 15-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- SSH creates an ENCRYPTED tunnel to a remote machine (unlike old Telnet, which was plain text)
- First connection to a new server asks to trust its fingerprint (saved in ~/.ssh/known_hosts)
- ssh user@host "command" runs ONE command remotely without a full interactive login
- scp copies files securely; needs -r for entire folders (like cp)
- who/w show who's currently logged into a machine
- SSH (port 22) is a very common brute-force target — this connects back to Day 7's auth.log

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ssh user@host (basic connection)
[ ] ssh user@host "command" (single remote command)
[ ] scp file.txt user@host:/path/
[ ] scp -r folder/ user@host:/path/
[ ] who / w

MY OWN OUTPUT (fill this in after running commands yourself):
$ ssh raven@<test-server-ip> "whoami"
> 

$ scp notes.txt raven@<test-server-ip>:/home/raven/
> 

$ who (on the remote machine)
> 


